    "entry": "simulation.json"
  },
  "i18n": {
    "en": {
      "name": "The support desk's settings viewer",
      "shortDescription": "You have inherited a small Lambda function the support desk uses. Change how you call it, find out what it can read, and submit the internal value you find.",
      "instructions": "## Getting started\n\nTenka Cloud's support desk uses a small Lambda function, the \"settings viewer\", to look up settings it may share with customers, such as support hours. You have just inherited this function. A note says the next release will add an internal notification feature to it.\n\nSecurity asks one thing: \"Run it and tell us whether it is safe to hand this function to the support desk.\"\n\n## Terms (only the ones in this problem)\n\n- **Lambda function** — a short program kept in the cloud that runs when called. This one is written in Python.\n- **Parameter Store** — a shelf of short named strings. Names are separated by slashes, like `/area/name`. Settings that may be shared live under `/<prefix>/public/`.\n- **Execution role** — the permissions a function uses while it runs. They are separate from the permissions of whoever calls it.\n- **Environment variables** — settings handed to the function in advance. They appear when you read the function's configuration.\n- **Flag** — a pass phrase that proves you got there. Here it is one line that starts with `TC{` and ends with `}`. It changes on every deploy.\n\n## First move\n\nThe problem page's **Deployment outputs (details)** show the function name `FunctionName` and `NamePrefix`. Get CLI credentials from the entry shown on the problem page (when the event runs on the local Simulator, use the CLI only). Below, `<function>` is the `FunctionName` value and `<prefix>` is the `NamePrefix` value.\n\n1. Call the function.\n\n```\naws lambda invoke --function-name <function> --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out --payload '{\"name\": \"support-hours\"}' out.json && cat out.json\n```\n\nYou are ready when the support hours come back. The returned `name` is the shelf entry the function actually read.\n\n2. Read the function's configuration and check its environment variables and execution role.\n\n```\naws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name <function>\n```\n\n3. With your own permissions you can read only under `/<prefix>/public/`. `aws ssm get-parameter --name /<prefix>/public/greeting` works, but other names are refused. Change how you call the function and find out what it can read for you.\n\n## Goal\n\nFind the internal value (one line starting with `TC{`), paste it into **Flag (deployment output value)** on the problem page and submit. A match earns +100 pt. Each wrong answer costs 5 pt.\n\n## One step further (not scored)\n\nAfter you find the value, try fixing the function. Save this starting point as `index.py` and add, where the comment is, a check that lets through only the names of settings that may be shared.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nimport boto3\n\nssm = boto3.client(\"ssm\")\nSETTINGS_PREFIX = os.environ[\"SETTINGS_PREFIX\"]\n\n\ndef handler(event, context):\n    name = str(event.get(\"name\", \"greeting\"))\n    # Check here that name is one of the settings that may be shared\n    full_name = SETTINGS_PREFIX + name\n    try:\n        value = ssm.get_parameter(Name=full_name)[\"Parameter\"][\"Value\"]\n    except Exception as error:\n        return {\"ok\": False, \"name\": full_name, \"error\": type(error).__name__}\n    return {\"ok\": True, \"name\": full_name, \"value\": value}\n```\n\nZip it and redeploy it to the function.\n\n```\nzip fixed.zip index.py\naws lambda update-function-code --function-name <function> --zip-file fileb://fixed.zip\n```\n\nYou are done when the call that gave you the flag now fails while `{\"name\": \"support-hours\"}` still returns the support hours. When the event runs on the local Simulator, no redeploy ever touches a real cloud.",
      "description": "A beginner problem about a Lambda function's execution role and input handling, in an environment you may break.\n\nThe deploy creates a support-desk Lambda function `{NamePrefix}-settings-viewer`, two public settings and an internal parameter `/{NamePrefix}/internal/release-token`. The function's execution role reads everything under `/{NamePrefix}/*`, and the function reads any name that starts with `/` as given. The participant's own role reads only the public settings directly, but through the function the internal value comes out. Submitting that value (`TC{…}`, random per deploy) is checked against the `ReleaseTokenValue` output for 100 pt.\n\nThe participant role may invoke the function, read its configuration, redeploy its code and read the execution role's policy, nothing more. As an unscored step, participants fix the code, redeploy it and confirm the same call now fails.\n\nOnly Lambda and SSM Standard tier are used: no EC2, VPC or public endpoint. The same template runs on AWS and on the local TenkaCloud Simulator.",
      "learningGoals": [
        "Experience that a Lambda function runs with its execution role, not with the caller's permissions",
        "Confirm that the function's input together with its role's scope reaches values the caller cannot read",
        "Fix the code, redeploy it and confirm yourself that the same call stops working"
      ],
      "hints": [
        {
          "id": "hint-1",
          "content": "A Lambda function reads Parameter Store with its execution role, not with yours. A name you cannot read with `aws ssm get-parameter` may still be readable by the function. Check whether the function's configuration (environment variables) lists a name the function knows."
        },
        {
          "id": "hint-2",
          "content": "Small example: if a function turns `{\"name\": \"hours\"}` into `/team/public/hours` but reads `{\"name\": \"/team/notes\"}` as `/team/notes`, the caller chooses the shelf. Look at the `name` the function returns for each call."
        },
        {
          "id": "hint-3",
          "content": "Copy the value of the environment variable `RELEASE_TOKEN_PARAM` (it starts with `/`). Call the function with `--payload '{\"name\": \"<that value>\"}'`. The returned `value` is the flag; paste it from `TC{` to `}`."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "scoring": {
    "kind": "flag",
    "flagOutputKey": "ReleaseTokenValue",
